  "full_response": "### **How a Hash Table Works**\n\nA **hash table** (also called a hash map) is a data structure that stores key-value pairs and provides efficient insertion, deletion, and lookup operations. It works by using a **hash function** to compute an index (or \"hash code\") into an array of **buckets** (or slots), where the desired value can be found.\n\n#### **Key Components:**\n1. **Hash Function**\n   - Takes a key as input and computes an integer (hash code) that determines the index where the value should be stored.\n   - A good hash function:\n     - Distributes keys uniformly across buckets (minimizes collisions).\n     - Is deterministic (same key → same hash).\n     - Is fast to compute.\n\n2. **Buckets (Array of Slots)**\n   - Each bucket holds a key-value pair (or a linked list/other structure in case of collisions).\n\n3. **Collision Handling**\n   - **Separate Chaining:** Each bucket contains a linked list (or tree) of entries that hash to the same index.\n   - **Open Addressing:** If a collision occurs, the algorithm probes for the next available slot (e.g., linear probing, quadratic probing, or double hashing).\n\n---\n\n### **Operations & Time Complexity**\n\n| **Operation** | **Average Case** | **Worst Case** | **Explanation** |\n|--------------|----------------|----------------|----------------|\n| **Insert**   | **O(1)**       | **O(n)**       | Hash function computes index in O(1). If collisions occur, insertion may degrade to O(n) in worst case (all keys collide). |\n| **Lookup**   | **O(1)**       | **O(n)**       | Hash function locates bucket in O(1). If many collisions, lookup may scan a linked list/tree (O(n) in worst case). |\n| **Delete**   | **O(1)**       | **O(n)**       | Similar to lookup; after finding the key, deletion is O(1) (or O(log n) if using a tree for chaining). |\n\n#### **Why Average Case is O(1)?**\n- A well-designed hash table with a good hash function and proper resizing (load factor management) ensures that most operations take constant time.\n- **Load Factor (λ)** = (Number of entries) / (Number of buckets)\n  - If λ > threshold (e.g., ",
